I"�<p>Many people have been asking me about this paddle because it looks interesting, mainly because I’ve used some fancy shirt buttons! :-)</p>

<iframe width="560" height="315" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/RtEYI4aF7j4" title="Metal clip paddle" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen=""></iframe>
<p>It’s really easy to make, and there’s a good chance you already have all the parts you need in your drawer.</p>

<p>You’ll need:</p>
<ul>
  <li>paper clip</li>
  <li>binder clip</li>
  <li>metal shirt buttons - solderable</li>
  <li>some plastic main body element - in my case a random plastic pushbutton</li>
  <li>magnet</li>
  <li>2 screws</li>
  <li>piece of PCB</li>
</ul>

<p><img src="/assets/images/clip1.jpg" alt="clip1" width="600" /></p>

<p>First, you need to prepare the binder clip.</p>

<p>File down the edge so that the binder clip handle can be pushed out.</p>

<p><img src="/assets/images/clip2.jpg" alt="clip2" width="600" />
<img src="/assets/images/clip3.jpg" alt="clip3" width="600" /></p>

<p>Now you can put everything together:</p>

<p><img src="/assets/images/clip4.jpg" alt="clip4" width="600" /></p>

<p>Use the paper clip to make arm limiters.</p>

<p>Solder the buttons to the binder clip handles.</p>

<p>Glue the PCB to the binder clip. These will act as the paddle contacts.</p>

<p>And there you have it — a cheap but surprisingly nice, fully functional paddle! :-)</p>

